
There is startapp, but it doesn't transfer stdin/stdout to the program.
For interactive use in clautolisp (and alfe), we introduce the shell escape
character "!".
It is a configurable value shell-escape-character

When a line is input at the REPL (any interactor, lisp, aldo, navi,
sedit), that starts with this shell escape character, the rest of the
line is passed to the $SHELL for interpretation, with
stdin/stdout/stderr inherited.

If the command ends in & the shell will fork a child process, but bash
doesn't display the PID or any job-control instruction in this
(non-interactive) mode. So we must add "echo $!" to the command to
print the pid of the child.

| Example interaction    | What is executed/printed     | Alternative output     |
|------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------|
| _$ !pwd                | bash -c 'pwd'                |                        |
| /Users/pjb/src/ogamita | /Users/pjb/src/ogamita       |                        |
|------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------|
| _$ !pwd &              | bash -c 'pwd & echo $!'      |                        |
| 4819                   | 4819                         | /Users/pjb/src/ogamita |
| /Users/pjb/src/ogamita | /Users/pjb/src/ogamita       | 4819                   |
|------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------|
| _$ !echo hello \       |                              |                        |
| > world.               | bash -c 'echo hello  world.' |                        |
| hello world.           | hello world.                 |                        |
|------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------|

There may not be any space or other character input before the
shell-escape-character.

There may be spaces before or after the shell command, so the test for
& must take into account this possibility.

There may be continuation lines when the preceeding line ends with a
backslash "\".

The "What is executed" column is only an example, it depends on $SHELL
environment variable.

* RESOLVED 2026-08-15 (clautolisp 1.8.37)

Implemented, with the setting in the LISP interactor's own
configuration, per pjb: "c'est une caractéristique de l'interactor lisp,
sa configuration est enregistrée dans la configuration de l'interactor
lisp. setting name: shell-escape-character, type: ascii character or
unicode code point."

** Two hooks, not code, and why

=clautolisp/autolisp-interactor= is deliberately dependency-free — its
own description says "No clautolisp dependencies, so it is reusable
outside the debugger". Running a subprocess would drag in uiop; reading
the setting would drag in the configuration layer, which sits ABOVE it.
So the interactor carries two hooks and no policy:

- =*SHELL-ESCAPE-CHARACTER-HOOK*= — no arguments, returns the character
  or NIL;
- =*SHELL-ESCAPE-RUNNER*= — called with the command string.

Both installed by the tool at start-up, exactly as
=*DEFAULT-ON-QUIT-POLICY*= is for sedit. Either being NIL disables the
escape, so an embedder that installs nothing sees no change at all.

The character is fetched through a FUNCTION rather than copied into a
variable at launch, so =,set shell-escape-character …= takes effect on
the next line rather than the next session.

** The detail that dictated the shape

"There may not be any space or other character input before the
shell-escape-character." The existing readers call =%READ-COMMAND-LINE=,
which LEFT-TRIMS — testing there would have accepted =" !pwd"= and
silently broken the rule. The readers now take the RAW line and trim
only after the escape test. That is the one place a naive implementation
goes wrong; there is a test for it.

** The rest of the rules, each with a test

- a trailing backslash continues onto the next line, and a backslash on
  the LAST line terminates instead of reading forever;
- a command ending in =&= gets = echo $!= appended, because a
  non-interactive bash prints neither the PID nor a job-control notice.
  Tested on the TRIMMED text since the ticket allows spaces on either
  side — and =a && b= does not end in =&=, so it is left alone;
- =$SHELL= (or =/bin/sh=) runs it with stdin/stdout/stderr INHERITED,
  which is the whole point: =startapp= exists and cannot do that;
- a failing shell command is reported, never signalled — a typo must not
  unwind the REPL that launched it.

** The setting

=shell-escape-character= in =lisp.conf=, default =!=. New setting type
=:character=, accepting a one-character string or a decimal code point —
the same two spellings the =:decorations= glyphs already accept in
=aldo.conf=, so the file keeps one convention rather than two. A code
point is how you name a character awkward to type. Values are normalised
on read by =SETTING-CHARACTER=, so a hand-edited
=(shell-escape-character . "@")= works as well as the character syntax,
and a malformed value disables the escape rather than breaking the REPL.

** Found while doing this: a whole test suite was never running

The new tests reported neither pass nor fail. =INTERACTOR-SUITE= was
absent from the aggregate =clautolisp/tests=: the interactor framework
had its own test system and its own suite, and nothing invoked them.
Now wired in — 190 checks, including the pre-existing ones that had
never run in CI.

** Scope note

The ticket says "any interactor, lisp, aldo, navi, sedit". The escape IS
active in all of them (both readers changed, both consulting the same
hook), but the character is read through =LISP-SETTING=, so it always
comes from =lisp.conf= — aldo cannot name a different one. That matches
pjb's narrowing; making it per-interactor is a one-line change to the
hook if ever wanted.

Verified: SBCL and CCL suites green (exit 0), the 8 new tests named in
the log.
